Yes.  And while we are a minority, we are a rapidly-growing one, as more
and more people discover the tricks that allow someone to turn the
post-Castle game into a no-brainer cakewalk _without_ overpreparation.

I'm not against overpreparation by any means (though I am against
effortless/automated overpreparation, obviously)... but when the
supposedly-hard endgame doesn't even _require_ the overpreparation to
breeze through?  There's some stuff wrong here, sorry.

> Color me not human, but I want a challenge, and vanilla nethack no longer
> provides. Sporkhack interests me because it may provide that challenge;
> admittedly I've still been playing Crawl instead, but I already _know_
> that will provide me with a challenge :) Sporkhack is likely not for
> everyone; I think there is a market for it, though, and that some of its 
> ideas might do vanilla good as well.

I would like Sporkhack to be for everyone, but obviously that will to
some degree always be a pipe dream; vanilla will never have its shortage
of die-hard adherents, and to those _all_ my changes will seem bad,
because I'm tampering with the One True Game.  That's fine; I don't
_need_ to appeal to everyone.

That said, I have made a good effort to make sure that my changes
primarily make it harder/more challenging in the Castle and post-Castle
levels, as opposed to the early game.  The curve on which Elbereth's
efficacy drops, for example, is one such case; there are others, and
other changes that are planned are _also_ intended to go in on the same
type of curve.

The early game is the one part of Nethack that is relatively universally
agreed to be both challenging and interesting.  I have no interest in
tampering with that too carelessly.

> I base this assertion on:
>
> "Not by Demogoron (was wearing an AoLS when he got me), though an arch-
> lich decided to invite Yeenoghu and Demogorgon over for a family get-
> together. Not by Rodney, who showed up approximately ten times (was
> wearing several different AoLS). The nasties simply waged a war of
> attrition. Thus my Sporkhack high score simply reads: killed by a
> stalker."

This, by the way, is exactly what my changes are intended to do; get
someone to either be clever, or burn resources that normally would 
be saved until Astral; and then there simply aren't enough monsters on Astral
in vanilla that can do damage to make you burn even a tenth of those
saved resources, even if you WEREN'T jumping past and teleporting 
through them all.

> But even given that --- with all due respect to 3cnfsat, I also don't
> think that he used all of the tactics available at his disposal, though
> it still sounds like he got a tough endgame.

Leaving the amulet behind accidentally is a big killer, IMO.  Time is 
valuable on the ascension run, even more so when Rodney is popping in
and out to hassle you.

> Finally, it was a genoless game --- a challenge, conduct ascension. 
> Genoless has plenty of teeth in vanilla, and I think this is ample
> evidence that it has even more in Sporkhack. 

This is surely true.  As stated elsewhere, I am not considering the
difficulty in conducts when balancing the game -- if genoless suddenly
moves from "relatively easy" in vanilla to "wow, that's a bitch" in
Spork, so be it.  The conducts will be judged relative to themselves,
much as they are now.

> It might still be misguided, but I don't think it's for the reasons you
> think it is. If it is misguided, I'm guessing it's because it's forcing
> players to burn resources but not the resources that matter. 

Yep.  If someone can burn resources that don't matter to solve a
problem, why was I making changes in the first place?

However... if someone can burn resources that don't matter _now_, but
matter _later_... then we're talkin'.  :)  Case in point: quaffing a
potion of speed will cure the new "Slow" status.  Big deal?  Maybe...
especially if you were going to alchemize with that later, or maybe
drink all of your remaining ones right before entering Astral.
Sometimes even the small changes can matter.

> they didn't take him all the way to the top. Perhaps sporkhack is not as
> solved a resource collection problem as vanilla! Or, perhaps it will be
> solved by some ingenious player who finds a hole in the balance system,

I can assure you right now that both of these will be true.  Most people
haven't even asked whether ?oSM was changed, or even tried it out; I
expect this will change as more people remember that it's a good
resource to have handy and blessed.

Also, I'm certain there will be at least one hole in the balance system
which can be exploited; as stated before, I have faith in the ingenuity
of Nethack players, so I'm not afraid to make changes that look harsh at
first.  These players will respond, and eventually one of them will find
a hole big enough to drive a truck through.

> which will then be corrected! Luckily, it's under active and rapid
> development for the moment, so Derek has the opportunity to respond to
> these kind of issues if and when they come up. 

This is probably the biggest advantage I have.  I can move much more quickly
as a single developer, and I can literally update the codebase underneath
players on the server if there's a major bug.  There are disadvantages
to this as well, but when you're trying to tweak/balance things, it's
certainly handy.  And, as some people have already discovered, I intend
to use it; I was able to patch around the "defunct monster" bug and
recover a couple people's savegames who were hit by it.

